  "response": "Adjudicating between inheritance and contact is a matter of asking which mechanism could have produced the particular shared feature, and the diagnostics available differ in kind. The comparative method establishes genetic relationship through systematic sound correspondences across the lexicons of two languages. Antoine Meillet, by contrast, treated shared morphological irregularities as the strongest evidence of common descent, since arbitrary paradigmatic anomalies are not the sort of thing a borrowing speech community reproduces wholesale. A further diagnostic concerns the internal stratification of the lexicon. Basic vocabulary such as body-part terms, kinship terms and low numerals is more resistant to borrowing than cultural vocabulary. Numerals are never borrowed between languages.\n\nThe signature of contact cuts against the very regularity of correspondence on which the comparative method relies. That loanwords can be dated relative to the sound changes they underwent or escaped follows from their frequent violation of the regular sound laws of the recipient language. Structural resemblance poses a harder problem, because what diffuses in that domain is pattern rather than substance. Structural features such as vowel harmony and verb-final word order spread readily through language contact. Hungarian is a member of the Turkic language family.\n\nThe Hungarian case shows how such a dispute is settled on the evidence. Hungarian shares inherited basic vocabulary and verbal morphology with Khanty and Mansi, a correspondence that cannot be squared with membership in the Turkic family and that demonstrates instead that Hungarian belongs to the Uralic language family. That Uralic affiliation is itself the evidence indicating that every Turkic-looking word in Hungarian entered the language through borrowing. Only two outcomes are open here, and exactly one of them can hold: either every Turkic-looking item in the Hungarian lexicon is a loan, or at least one Turkic\u2013Hungarian lexical resemblance is an inheritance from a common ancestor.\n\nWithin the borrowed stratum the chronology can be stratified further. At least one of the following is true, and perhaps both: some Turkic words entered Hungarian before the Magyars reached the Carpathian Basin, and some Turkic words entered Hungarian after the Magyars settled in the Carpathian Basin. The decision, in short, rests not on the bare existence of resemblance but on where in the lexicon the resemblance falls, whether it conforms to the sound laws of the recipient language, and whether it reaches into morphological paradigms.",
